# Call-based inference, and the type variables it works out.
#
# Nothing here is annotated. `map` is worked out the first time
# something calls it, and the parameter types that call passed are
# carried through its body -- but *marked*, so that what the body did
# to each can be told from what it happened to be. What the body never
# looked at is what a type variable can stand for.
#
# `map` is first called with `double` and a list of Ints. Its body pulls
# the list apart, so it has to be a list; it hands elements to `f` and
# collects what comes back, and it never asks what either of those is.
# So `map` is worked out as
#
#     ((a) -> b, [a]) -> [b]
#
# which is its most general type, and the reason the second call --
# `label`, which answers with a String -- goes through the same
# definition without it being checked again.
#
# `double` is the other half. Its body multiplies, and `*` asks what its
# operands are, so nothing about `double` is a variable: it is
# `(Int) -> Int` and nothing more general would be true.
#
# The signature the marks propose is not taken on trust. It is checked
# against the body again, with its variables rigid -- the same check an
# annotated polymorphic function gets -- so a proposal the marks got
# wrong costs generality and never soundness.

def map(f, lst):
    match lst:
        case [x, ..xs]:
            [f(x)] ++ map(f, xs)
        case []:
            []

def double(n): n * 2

def label(n): 'n=' ++ string(n)

numbers = [1, 4, 6, 9]

print(map(double, numbers))
print(map(label, numbers))

# The same definition again, at a third element type -- `map` never asks
# what it is carrying.
print(map(double, [10, 20]))

# Bound so the printed AST shows the types themselves.
mapping = map
doubling = double
